  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
